  "verdict": "[CORRECTED PER CODEX R30 REVIEW -- this is a REDUCED-SCALE HYPOTHESIS, not the production proof] At batch 16 (num_workers=0, seed=0), through the IDENTICAL RLinf model+loop+noise/time, RLinf's own loader sequence descends faster (last-5 0.0739) than the reference loader sequence (last-5 0.1616), and the step-0 gap on identical weights+noise/time (0.165 vs 0.251) isolates that difference to the batch. This is DIRECTIONAL evidence that loader batch composition changes the trajectory under a shared loop. It is NOT the decisive production proof: the reference-loader trajectory here (0.162) does NOT track the reference production curve (r24 ~0.090), and the recipe is not the production global-256 / seed-42 / num_workers-8 one. The production-faithful replay is R31. Do NOT mark task15 met on this artifact.",
  "key_finding_step0": "On IDENTICAL base weights + IDENTICAL shared noise/time, step 0 loss is ref-loader 0.2512 vs rlinf-loader 0.1654 -- since weights+noise/time are identical, this difference is PURELY the batch (data), cleanly proving the two loaders feed different per-step inputs (no noise/time-RNG confound, unlike the earlier R27 step-0 observation).",
  "likely_mechanism_hypothesis": "RLinf's BEHAVIOR SFT loader streams CONTIGUOUS keyframe chunks (AC-6 streaming sharding), so its per-step batches are more correlated (same-episode frames) and fit faster, whereas the reference create_behavior_data_loader_torch shuffles more globally -> more diverse, harder batches. R19 established the two loaders draw the SAME frame corpus; the difference is the per-step grouping/order. This is a HYPOTHESIS for the mechanism (not separately proven this round); the MEASURED fact is that the loader batch composition drives the descent-rate difference.",
  "ac11_path_proposal": "DEFERRED to R31 (the production-faithful global-256 replay). No AC-11 plan evolution is proposed on this reduced-scale artifact.",
  "status_correction_R31": "REDUCED-SCALE HYPOTHESIS, NOT the production proof (corrected per Codex R30 review). This replay is single-GPU at batch 16 with num_workers=0 and seed=0 -- NOT the production global batch 256 / seed=42 / num_workers=8 recipe -- and the reference-loader trajectory here (last-5 0.162) does NOT track the committed reference production curve (r24 ~0.090 @step49). So R30 only shows DIRECTIONALLY that loader batch composition can change the trajectory under a shared loop; it does not prove the production faster-descent is fully root-caused to the loader. The production-faithful global-256 replay is R31 (docs/evidence/r31_loader_sequence_prod.json). task15 stays OPEN."
